<figure class="pipeline">
  <div class="pipeline-label">One 16 MiB catch-up response</div>

  <div class="paths">
    <article class="path before">
      <header>
        <span class="tag">Before</span>
        <strong>Read and hold the complete suffix</strong>
      </header>

      <div class="flow">
        <div class="station redis">
          <span class="station-label">Redis</span>
          <div class="suffix" aria-hidden="true">
            <i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i>
          </div>
          <b>unbounded suffix</b>
          <small>one Lua reply</small>
        </div>

        <span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span>

        <div class="station server">
          <span class="station-label">Chronicle</span>
          <div class="buffers" aria-hidden="true">
            <i>storage result</i>
            <i>response body</i>
          </div>
          <b>two full buffers</b>
          <small>before the first write</small>
        </div>

        <span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span>

        <div class="station client">
          <span class="station-label">Client</span>
          <div class="socket old-socket" aria-hidden="true"></div>
          <b>first byte waits</b>
          <small>for the suffix read</small>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p>
        Redis materializes every remaining frame. Chronicle then builds another
        complete body before it writes to the socket.
      </p>
    </article>

    <article class="path after">
      <header>
        <span class="tag">After</span>
        <strong>Capture one tail and drain bounded pages</strong>
      </header>

      <div class="snapshot">
        <span>snapshot tail</span>
        <code>…4096</code>
        <span>incarnation</span>
        <code>7f3c…</code>
      </div>

      <div class="flow">
        <div class="station redis">
          <span class="station-label">Redis</span>
          <div class="pages" aria-hidden="true">
            <i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i>
            <em>×16</em>
          </div>
          <b>first + bounded bulk</b>
          <small>≤1,024 frames per page</small>
        </div>

        <span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span>

        <div class="station server">
          <span class="station-label">Chronicle</span>
          <div class="page-buffer" aria-hidden="true">
            <i></i><span>about 1 MiB</span>
          </div>
          <b>one returned page</b>
          <small>held at a time</small>
        </div>

        <span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span>

        <div class="station client">
          <span class="station-label">Client</span>
          <div class="socket new-socket" aria-hidden="true">
            <i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i>
          </div>
          <b>4 KiB writes</b>
          <small>flush while paging</small>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p>
        Every page stays below the captured tail. A later append cannot enter
        the response, and a delete followed by recreate cannot reuse the old
        snapshot.
      </p>
    </article>
  </div>

  <figcaption>
    The 1 MiB target applies to the returned whole-frame prefix. A first
    indivisible frame can exceed it. The separate 1,024-frame
    <code>LIMIT</code> bounds small-frame pages, and the script never fetches a
    lookahead frame.
  </figcaption>
</figure>

